hadiths[22]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1806","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every prophet was given such miracles that people believed in him because of them. And the miracle which has been given to me is the Revelation (the Qur'an) which Allah has revealed to me. (The effect of all previous miracles ended with time, but this miracle (the Qur'an) is everlasting.) So I hope that my followers will outnumber the followers of all other prophets on the Day of Resurrection."""
"1807","Narrated from Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): Allah sent down revelation to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) frequently before his death, until near his death, revelation came in abundance. Then after that, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away."
"1808","Ameer-ul-Mu'mineen Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I heard Sayyiduna Hisham bin Hakeem (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting Surah Al-Furqan during the blessed life of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I listened attentively and noticed that he was reciting it in several ways different from those in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taught me this Surah. (I became angry) and was about to attack him during the prayer itself, but I controlled myself. When he finished the prayer with salam, I put my cloak around his neck and asked, ""Who taught you this Surah in the way I heard you recite it?"" He replied, ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me."" I said, ""You are lying! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself taught me this Surah in a different way."" Then I dragged him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah! I heard him reciting Surah Al-Furqan in a way you did not teach me."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""Let him go (and said), O Hisham! Recite."" He recited it in the same way I had heard him recite. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""This Surah was revealed in this way."" Then he said, ""O Umar! Now you recite."" I recited it in the way the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taught me, and he said, ""It was revealed in this way as well. Indeed, this Qur'an has been revealed in seven ways (dialects), so recite in whichever way is easy for you."""
"1809","Lady Fatimah al-Zahra (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me quietly (in a low voice): ""Jibreel (peace be upon him) used to review the Qur'an with me once every year, and this year he reviewed it twice. I think that the time of my death has approached."""
"1810","`Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with both of them) says that` By Allah, I have learned more than seventy surahs of the Qur'an directly from the blessed mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1811","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was in (the Syrian city of) Homs. There he recited Surah Yusuf, so a person said, ""This Surah was not revealed in the way you have recited it."" Sayyiduna Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""I recited this Surah before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: 'You have recited very well.'"" And (Sayyiduna Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him) perceived the smell of wine from his mouth, so he said: ""On one hand, you deny the Book of Allah, and on the other, you enjoy the pleasures of wine?"" Then he imposed the prescribed punishment on him."
"1812","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him): A man heard someone reciting ""(Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad)"" (Surah Al-Ikhlas) repeatedly. In the morning, he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned this, as if he considered ""(Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad)"" to be little (in reward) due to its few words. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul! Verily, this Surah '(Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad)' is equal to one third of the Qur'an."""
"1813","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions (by way of rhetorical question): ""Is it difficult for any one of you to recite one third of the Qur'an in a single night?"" This seemed difficult to them and they said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Who among us has the strength to do that?"" So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Surah Al-Ikhlas, in which the attributes 'Allah is One, the Self-Sufficient' are mentioned, is equal to one third of the Qur'an."""
"1814","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that whenever the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) would go to his bed every night, he would join his two palms together and recite (Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad), (Qul A’udhu bi-Rabbil-Falaq), and (Qul A’udhu bi-Rabbin-Nas) over them, then blow into them and wipe them over his entire body as far as he could reach. He would start by wiping his blessed head and face, and then the front part of his body, and he would do this three times."
"1815","Sayyiduna Usaid bin Hudair (may Allah be pleased with him) says that he was reciting Surah Al-Baqarah one night and his horse was tied near him. Suddenly, the horse began to startle. When he became silent, the horse calmed down. He started reciting again, and the horse startled again. He became silent again, and the horse calmed down. He started reciting again, and the horse startled again. Then he stopped (ceased recitation), and his son Yahya was lying near the horse. He feared that the horse might trample him. When he moved him from there and looked towards the sky, the sky was not visible. In the morning, he came and related this to the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Ibn Hudair! You should have continued reciting, O Ibn Hudair! You should have continued reciting."" He said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Yahya was lying near the horse, and I feared that the horse might trample Yahya, so I raised my head and returned to him. Then I raised my head towards the sky and saw a strange canopy-like cloud in which many lamps were lit. Then I went outside until it disappeared from my sight."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do you know what that was?"" He said, ""No."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Those were angels who had come near you upon hearing your voice. If you had continued reciting till morning, other people would also have seen them (openly), and they would not have disappeared from their sight."""
"1816","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Envy is permitted only in two cases: one, for the person whom Allah has given the Qur'an and he recites it during the night and day, so his neighbor hears him and says, 'I wish I had been given what he has been given, so I would do as he does.' And the second, for the person whom Allah has given wealth and he spends it in the way of truth, then someone (out of envy) says, 'I wish I had been given wealth like so-and-so, so I would spend as he does in the way of truth.'"""
"1817","Narrated from Sayyiduna Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The best among you is the one who learns the Qur'an and teaches it."""
"1818","Sayyiduna Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) in another narration says that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The best among you is the one who learns the Qur'an and teaches it."""
"1819","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The example of the one who memorizes and recites the Qur'an is like the owner of camels tied with a rope; if he takes care of them, he will keep them, and if he neglects them, they will run away."""
"1820","Narrated Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is bad that any of you says, 'I have forgotten such-and-such verse.' Rather, he should say, 'I have been made to forget it.' And keep reciting the Qur'an, for it escapes from the hearts of men faster than camels do."""
"1821","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Keep reciting the Qur'an regularly. By the One in Whose hand is my soul! The Qur'an escapes from the hearts of men faster than a camel that breaks its rope and runs away."""
"1822","It was asked from Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): ""How was the recitation of the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"" He said: He used to recite by elongating (stretching the words). Then (Sayyiduna Anas, may Allah be pleased with him) recited ""Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem"" — in it, he elongated ""Bismillah"" and also elongated ""ar-Rahman"" and ""ar-Raheem"" (recited them by stretching)."
"1823","Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""O Abu Musa! You have been given one of the sweet voices from the sweet voices of the family of Dawud."""
"1824","`Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) says that` my father (Sayyiduna Amr bin As) (may Allah be pleased with him) married me to a woman from a good family, and he would often ask his daughter-in-law about her husband (me), and she would reply, ""Yes, yes, he is a good and pious man, but since I have come, he has never set foot on my bedding nor come near me."" When a period of time had passed, (my father) mentioned this to the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him), so he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Bring him to me."" I (upon being called) went to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he (peace and blessings be upon him) asked, ""How do you fast?"" I said, ""Daily."" Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) asked, ""How do you complete the Qur'an?"" I said, ""Every night."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Fast three days in every month and complete the Qur'an once in a month."" I said, ""I have the strength for more than that."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Then fast three days in a week."" I said, ""I have the strength for more than that."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Then fast two days and break the fast one day."" I said, ""I have the strength for more than that."" So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Alright! Adopt the best fast, the fast of Dawud (peace be upon him), that is, fast one day and break the fast one day, and complete the Qur'an in seven nights."" (Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) says) I wish I had accepted the concession of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), because now I have become old and weak (I no longer have that strength). Then he would have one-seventh (one manzil) of the Qur'an recited to someone from his family during the day. The portion that was to be recited at night, he would repeat during the day so that it would be easier to recite at night, and when he became weak and wanted to regain strength, he would not fast continuously for several days, then, counting those days, he would make up the fasts. He disliked that any matter should remain which he used to do in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."
"1825","`Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""A people will emerge from among you such that you will consider your prayers insignificant compared to their prayers, and your fasts compared to their fasts, and your (other) good deeds compared to their deeds. And they will recite the Qur'an, but it will not go beyond their throats. They will go out of the religion just as an arrow passes out of the hunted animal, so that the hunter finds nothing on the arrowhead, nor anything stuck to the shaft, nor any trace on the feathers—just a slight doubt (if the hunter looks at the arrowhead, he sees nothing on it)."""
"1826","Narrated Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: “The example of the believer who recites the Qur’an and acts upon it is like that of a citron, whose taste is good and whose fragrance is good. And the Muslim who does not recite the Qur’an but acts upon it is like a date, whose taste is good but it has no fragrance. And the example of the hypocrite who recites the Qur’an is like that of basil, whose fragrance is good but its taste is bitter. And the example of the hypocrite who does not recite the Qur’an is like the colocynth, whose taste is bitter and bad and whose fragrance is also foul.”"
"1827","Narrated by Sayyiduna Jundub bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""Recite the Noble Qur'an as long as your heart is inclined towards it, and when you begin to differ among yourselves, then stop reciting the Qur'an."" (That is, due to lack of interest, the words may get mixed up or recited incorrectly.)"
